  "worked_solution": "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}-\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}\u2061(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x).\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy,\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(2xy)=2x \\\\ \\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}-\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}) & =2x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we conclude that an integrating factor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}\u2061(\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}\u2061({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the differential equation by the integrating factor gives\n\n$$\n{e}^{{x}^{2}}(2xy\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$",
